## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token so blocking can be interrupted
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Cancellation requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed. Loop condition will exit naturally.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerThread is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for graceful shutdown
        if (!_workerThread.Join(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2)))
        {
            // Thread didn't stop — it's stuck in uncooperative code.
            // Log a warning; you cannot force-kill it in .NET 8.
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Key Changes Explained

| Original | Migrated | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `volatile bool _running` | `CancellationTokenSource` + `CancellationToken` | Token integrates with blocking APIs; a bool cannot interrupt `Thread.Sleep` or blocked I/O |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | `Thread.Abort` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on .NET 6+ |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` | The `when` guard prevents swallowing unrelated OCEs |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | *(removed)* | Not needed — `OperationCanceledException` doesn't auto-rethrow like `ThreadAbortException` did |
| `Thread.Sleep(2000)` | `_workerThread.Join(2s)` | Actually waits for the thread instead of blindly sleeping |
| — | `IDisposable` | `CancellationTokenSource` must be disposed to avoid timer/callback leaks |

## Critical Pitfall: `GetNextItem()` Must Cooperate

The original comment says `GetNextItem()` "may block." **This is the hardest part.** You must pass the token into it so cancellation can interrupt the block:

```csharp
// If it's reading from a BlockingCollection<T>:
var item = _queue.Take(token);          // throws OCE on cancel

// If it's doing sync I/O (e.g., database poll):
// Replace with async, or use token.WaitHandle:
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(pollInterval); // wakes on cancel
if (token.IsCancellationRequested) throw new OperationCanceledException(token);

// If it calls Thread.Sleep internally:
// Replace Thread.Sleep(ms) with:
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms);           // cancellation-aware sleep
```

If `GetNextItem()` calls **third-party code you can't modify** and it blocks indefinitely, move that work to a child `Process` and use `Process.Kill()` — that's the only way to force-stop uncooperative code in .NET 8.

## Pitfalls to Watch

1. **Token must flow through the entire call chain** — if `ProcessItem` does long work internally, pass and check the token there too, or cancellation stalls.
2. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`** — let it propagate. Only catch at the top-level loop as shown.
3. **`CancellationToken` won't interrupt raw `socket.Receive()` or `Console.ReadLine()`** — replace with async overloads that accept a token.
4. **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** — leaking it causes timer leaks when `CancelAfter` is used.
